{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "publishedOn": "2026-08-28",
  "purpose": "Durable identity for every legal question the all-51 register has ever carried. The register used to assign Q-nnn by array position, so any change to the set of open questions silently re-pointed every id after the first change \u2014 thirty-seven of fifty-six ids changed meaning when the ratification correction removed thirty-nine routes from the legal-review queue. Q-004 stopped being one Kentucky question and became another; Q-011 stopped being a Maine question and became a Missouri one. The national-report crosswalk states the rule this file enforces: position in an array is not identity.",
  "rule": "A question's id is keyed to its track and its exact text. An id is never reused for a different question, and a question never changes id because the set around it changed. A question that leaves the live register keeps its id here, because the crosswalk and every decision record that cites it still mean the question it named.",
  "highestAssigned": 56,
  "count": 56,
  "ids": {
    "ga-rfo||Whether counsel approves a distinct post-consent packet stage for the \u00a7 42-8-66 petition \u2014 that is, a custom_pleading unit that generates the petition only after the participant has obtained the prosecuting attorney's written consent. The petition itself is legally identifiable and the framework is recorded on this track. The adopted memorandum makes the stage conditional on counsel's later approval, which has not been given, so direct delivery remains disabled.": "Q-001",
    "ky_felony_expungement_after_pardon||Whether the pardon instrument is a full pardon is a legal reading of a document LegalEase does not see. The packet asks the participant what their document says and routes to counsel on anything other than a plain full pardon, but the characterisation should be reviewed before release.": "Q-002",
    "ky_felony_vacatur_expungement||At least one circuit clerk's published page states that $300 is due at filing, which does not match KRS 431.073(10) and (11). The packet must state the statutory position and warn that a local office may ask for the whole sum at once.": "Q-003",
    "ky_nonconviction_expungement||Confirmation from the Administrative Office of the Courts of the intended completion of the AOC-497.2 eligibility checkboxes. The form instructs the filer to check all that apply and the five boxes describe mutually exclusive dispositions of a charge, so the disjunctive reading is the only workable one; the Clerks' Manual states the same periods disjunctively. Packet identity and the field map are settled and this does not stop the build.": "Q-004",
    "la-977d-marijuana-first-offense||Whether any clerk of court continues to apply the terminated Article 983(M) three-hundred-dollar schedule to motions filed after 1 August 2026, and whether any parish publishes a different collection practice for Article 977(D) motions.": "Q-005",
    "la-978-felony-conviction||Whether more than one felony conviction may be expunged within a single ten-year period, and on what showing each must independently qualify. The review states it is permitted where each is independently eligible under Article 978, and the current text does not address the point expressly.": "Q-006",
    "la-985-3-immediate-expungement||Whether Article 985.3 displaces the Article 977(A)(2) five-year and Article 978(A)(2) ten-year waiting periods for a person who is otherwise eligible and has successfully completed a court-ordered probation or alternative sentencing programme. The word 'immediate' and the completion trigger suggest it does, but unlike Article 985.1(D) \u2014 which disapplies those time limitations in terms \u2014 Article 985.3 says nothing about them, and 'otherwise eligible for an expungement' can be read either as eligible but for the waiting period or as eligible including the waiting period. The difference decides who the eligibility engine returns.": "Q-007",
    "la-985-expungement-by-redaction||Whether the Article 989 motion requires any distinct pleading or election to request redaction relief under Article 985 rather than full expungement, given that the redaction paragraph appears in the Article 992 order rather than in the motion, and that Article 985(A) speaks of petitioning the court.": "Q-008",
    "me-nonconv||Whether a charge dismissed after successful completion of a deferred disposition under 17-A M.R.S. ch. 67 subch. 4 is confidential criminal history record information under 16 M.R.S. \u00a7 703(2). This was checked against current official text and is NOT ESTABLISHED. Section 703(2) lists paragraphs A through L and none mentions a deferred disposition. The closest is paragraph G, which reaches a charge 'dismissed with prejudice by the court or dismissed with finality by a prosecutor outside a plea agreement'. Under 17-A M.R.S. \u00a7 1903 the State dismisses the charging instrument WITH PREJUDICE on compliance, which fits the first clause, but a deferred disposition arises from a written agreement between the defendant and the State, which arguably falls inside paragraph G's outside-a-plea-agreement carve-out. Section 1903 says nothing about confidentiality or sealing. No statute, State Bureau of Identification guidance, Judicial Branch page or Law Court decision resolves it. The packet therefore fails closed: it does not promise confidentiality on the strength of the deferred-disposition label, it tells the participant to verify what the Bureau record actually shows, and it routes the correction request only where the record is demonstrably inaccurate.": "Q-009",
    "me-seal-gen||Whether the movant must serve the prosecutorial office, or whether the court performs all notice. 15 M.R.S. \u00a7 2264(3) directs notice to the prosecutorial office that represented the State but does not assign the act to the movant, and none of CR-218, CR-289 or CR-307 carries a certificate-of-service block \u2014 unlike the juvenile form JV-043, which states on its face that the petitioner must provide a copy to the prosecuting attorney. The packet fails closed: it tells the participant that the court is understood to give notice, and that they should confirm with the clerk at filing and deliver a courtesy copy if the clerk asks.": "Q-010",
    "me-seal-survivor||Form CR-308 page 2 carries a warning box stating that if at any time after the date of the order the defendant is convicted of a new crime in Maine or in any other jurisdiction, the new conviction extinguishes the order. That is inconsistent with 15 M.R.S. \u00a7 2264(7) as amended by PL 2025, c. 513, which now provides in terms that the subsection does not apply to records sealed pursuant to section 2262-A or 2262-B. Both the form and the amendment take effect 29 July 2026, so this is a live conflict on the face of two current official sources rather than a stale-form problem. LegalEase does not resolve it: the packet states the statutory rule, does not repeat the form's warning as advice, and carries the conflict as a release blocker.": "Q-011",
    "me-seal-survivor||Whether the movant must serve the prosecutorial office, or whether the court performs all notice. 15 M.R.S. \u00a7 2264(3) directs notice to the prosecutorial office that represented the State but does not assign the act to the movant, and none of CR-218, CR-289 or CR-307 carries a certificate-of-service block \u2014 unlike the juvenile form JV-043, which states on its face that the petitioner must provide a copy to the prosecuting attorney. The packet fails closed: it tells the participant that the court is understood to give notice, and that they should confirm with the clerk at filing and deliver a courtesy copy if the clerk asks.": "Q-012",
    "mo-311-326-minor-in-possession||Which FI-05 case type code a \u00a7 311.326 application takes where a new case is opened. The published Case Types List names no code for the section. The code is left blank and confirmed with the clerk.": "Q-013",
    "mo-311-326-minor-in-possession||Whether a conviction under a municipal or county underage-possession ordinance, rather than under \u00a7 311.325 itself, is within \u00a7 311.326. The section names the statute and the packet applies the narrower reading, which excludes ordinance convictions; but many Missouri underage-drinking cases are charged municipally, so the narrower reading takes a substantial population out of the route. Unresolved on the face of the section.": "Q-014",
    "mo-610-130-first-intoxication||Which FI-05 case type code a \u00a7 610.130 application takes where a new case is opened. The official Case Types List publishes only XG, X5 and X# for expungement and none of them names this section. The code is left blank and confirmed with the clerk rather than guessed from a similar track name.": "Q-015",
    "mo-610-140-conviction||How a participant with cases charged in more than one Missouri county obtains complete relief. \u00a7 610.140.2(2) requires every crime sought to be expunged to be listed in the one petition, while \u00a7 610.140.2(1) fixes venue by the court where the person was charged or found guilty; the two cannot both be satisfied across counties. The review records multi-county records as a referral trigger and the track treats it as a hard self-help stop rather than resolving it.": "Q-016",
    "mo-610-145-mistaken-identity||Whether a participant may invoke \u00a7 610.145.1(2) where the prosecutor or judicial officer who ordered the dismissal did not give the required notice. The subsection imposes a duty on them and gives the participant no mechanism to enforce it; the practical answer is to petition under subdivision (1) instead, which the packet does, but whether any lesser step \u2014 a letter to the prosecutor, a request to the clerk \u2014 is the expected course is not addressed anywhere in the section.": "Q-017",
    "ms-misd-addl||Whether the Miss. Code Ann. \u00a7 99-19-72 fee reaches a given petition. The section reads: 'A filing fee of One Hundred Fifty Dollars ($150.00) is hereby levied on each petition to expunge an offense under Section 99-19-71 to be collected by the circuit clerk', distributed $100 to the Judicial System Operation Fund, $40 to the District Attorneys Operation Fund and $10 retained by the circuit clerk. Last amended by Laws, 1st Ex. Sess. 2017, ch. 7, \u00a7 12; no enacted amendment through 2026. Two things remain unresolved on its face and are NOT resolved by construction here. First, the trigger is 'each petition to expunge AN OFFENSE under Section 99-19-71': a \u00a7 99-19-71(4) non-conviction petition is a petition under \u00a7 99-19-71 but is not a petition to expunge an offence, because there is no conviction, and the statute does not settle it either way. Second, the collection mechanism names the CIRCUIT CLERK, which does not map onto \u00a7 99-19-71(1) or (4) petitions filed in justice or municipal court. Sections 9-11-15(3), 21-23-7(6), 99-15-26, 63-11-30, 41-29-150 and 67-3-70 sit outside \u00a7 99-19-71 and the fee does not reach them by its terms; none of those sections specifies a fee of its own. The packet therefore publishes NO fee amount on any track and instructs the participant to confirm the filing fee, and any pauper's-affidavit route, with the clerk of the court before filing.": "Q-018",
    "nd-dui-record-seal||N.D.C.C. \u00a7 39-08-01.6(1) says the court shall seal, and states no motion or petition trigger, whereas the neighbouring marijuana provision says expressly upon motion. The petition requirement therefore rests on the official ND Courts research guide rather than on the statutory text, and participant copy must not represent the petition as statutorily mandated.": "Q-019",
    "nd-dui-record-seal||The exclusion of impaired-driving offences from Chapter 12-60.1 appears only in the official ND Courts research guides and not in the statutory exclusions at N.D.C.C. \u00a7 12-60.1-02(2), which list only violence-or-intimidation felonies inside the firearm-disability period and registration offences. The routing to the separate \u00a7 39-08-01.6 seal follows the official guides, but the basis should be confirmed before release.": "Q-020",
    "nd-nonconviction-auto-close-verify||What remedy exists where the court does not close the record at day sixty-one. Neither N.D.C.C. \u00a7 12-60.1-05 nor the official instructions nor the research guide identifies one, so the packet can tell the participant to contact the clerk but cannot tell them what to do if that fails.": "Q-021",
    "nd-nonconviction-auto-close-verify||Whether the participant receives any notice that the record has been closed. Nothing in the statute or the official publications says so, which is why the packet builds the verification step around the public access search instead.": "Q-022",
    "ne-expunge-le-error||Whether venue is the district court of the county of arrest or any district court. The review states the county of arrest; the statutory text says only the district court. This must be closed before release.": "Q-023",
    "ne-expunge-le-error||Whether the county attorney is the named respondent, and the method and timing of service on a political subdivision. The review asserts the respondent; the statutory text does not.": "Q-024",
    "ne-expunge-le-error||Whether a summons or praecipe and a proposed order accompany the petition, which decides the packet component list.": "Q-025",
    "ne-seal-pardoned||The filing fee for a petition or motion filed within an existing Nebraska criminal case is not established. The statewide Filing Fees and Court Costs schedule effective 1 July 2026 carries no line item for post-judgment petitions or motions within a criminal case. No fee figure may be displayed to a participant until a clerk-level answer is obtained from at least one county court and one district court.": "Q-026",
    "ne-seal-pardoned||The fee-waiver branch has no fitting official form. DC 6:7.1's case-type box offers only Civil, Name change and Emancipation, and its official title is scoped to civil, appeals and emancipation matters, while a set-aside petition or sealing motion is filed in the existing criminal case. There is no county-court in forma pauperis application form at all: CC 8:1 is the order only. Neb. Rev. Stat. \u00a7 25-2301.01 nonetheless permits proceeding in forma pauperis in a civil or criminal case in any county or state court, so the mechanism exists and the form does not fit it. A custom affidavit and application under \u00a7\u00a7 25-2301 to 25-2310 is the resolution rather than mis-scoping DC 6:7.1.": "Q-027",
    "ne-setaside-custodial||The filing fee for a petition or motion filed within an existing Nebraska criminal case is not established. The statewide Filing Fees and Court Costs schedule effective 1 July 2026 carries no line item for post-judgment petitions or motions within a criminal case. No fee figure may be displayed to a participant until a clerk-level answer is obtained from at least one county court and one district court.": "Q-028",
