    "sc_17_1_40_general_sessions||The single-incident fee. Section 17-22-940(G) as amended by 2018 Act No. 254 was read at source on 2026-08-06 and provides that where multiple charges from a single incident are combined under \u00a7 17-1-40 or \u00a7 17-22-150(a), 'only one two hundred fifty-dollar fee, one twenty-five dollar SLED verification fee, and one thirty-five dollar clerk of court filing fee may be charged'. The controlling review reports the single-incident solicitor fee as one hundred fifty dollars, drawn from Supreme Court expungement guidance. The current statutory figure is two hundred fifty dollars. The packet quotes the statutory figure and does not promise the lower one.": "Q-033",
    "sc_17_1_65_handgun||The single-incident fee. Section 17-22-940(G) as amended by 2018 Act No. 254 was read at source on 2026-08-06 and provides that where multiple charges from a single incident are combined under \u00a7 17-1-40 or \u00a7 17-22-150(a), 'only one two hundred fifty-dollar fee, one twenty-five dollar SLED verification fee, and one thirty-five dollar clerk of court filing fee may be charged'. The controlling review reports the single-incident solicitor fee as one hundred fifty dollars, drawn from Supreme Court expungement guidance. The current statutory figure is two hundred fifty dollars. The packet quotes the statutory figure and does not promise the lower one.": "Q-034",
    "sc_17_22_950_summary||What a participant does where the automatic order under \u00a7 17-22-950(A) did not issue. The section was read in full at source on 2026-08-06 and imposes the duty on the summary court in mandatory terms, with the timing fixed by subsection (C), but it describes no application, motion or remedy on the fingerprinted branch where the court simply does not act. SCCA 223E is expressly the subsection (B) application and is not drafted for this situation. Until counsel resolves it the packet directs the participant to the summary court clerk and stops there; no enforcement filing is generated.": "Q-035",
    "sc_22_5_910||The single-incident fee. Section 17-22-940(G) as amended by 2018 Act No. 254 was read at source on 2026-08-06 and provides that where multiple charges from a single incident are combined under \u00a7 17-1-40 or \u00a7 17-22-150(a), 'only one two hundred fifty-dollar fee, one twenty-five dollar SLED verification fee, and one thirty-five dollar clerk of court filing fee may be charged'. The controlling review reports the single-incident solicitor fee as one hundred fifty dollars, drawn from Supreme Court expungement guidance. The current statutory figure is two hundred fifty dollars. The packet quotes the statutory figure and does not promise the lower one.": "Q-036",
    "sc_22_5_910||Whether the \u00a7 22-5-910(E) same-incident aggregation rule can be relied on in practice to bring several closely connected offences sentenced at one proceeding within a single application, given that \u00a7 17-22-940(G) permits multiple charges on one order only for \u00a7 17-1-40 and \u00a7 17-22-150(a). Both subsections were read at source on 2026-08-06 and are not obviously reconciled: \u00a7 22-5-910(E) treats such offences as one conviction 'for expungement purposes', while \u00a7 17-22-940(G) restricts what may appear on one order. The packet does not promise a single application on this route.": "Q-037",
    "sc_22_5_920_yoa||The single-incident fee. Section 17-22-940(G) as amended by 2018 Act No. 254 was read at source on 2026-08-06 and provides that where multiple charges from a single incident are combined under \u00a7 17-1-40 or \u00a7 17-22-150(a), 'only one two hundred fifty-dollar fee, one twenty-five dollar SLED verification fee, and one thirty-five dollar clerk of court filing fee may be charged'. The controlling review reports the single-incident solicitor fee as one hundred fifty dollars, drawn from Supreme Court expungement guidance. The current statutory figure is two hundred fifty dollars. The packet quotes the statutory figure and does not promise the lower one.": "Q-038",
