    "sd_sis_sealing||Counsel flagged as a release blocker whether sealing under \u00a7 23A-27-17 is automatic on discharge or requires a motion, and left an official_pdf_fill fallback unresolved against the possibility that a motion is needed. The section has now been read at the official South Dakota Legislature publication: 'Upon the discharge and dismissal of a person pursuant to \u00a7 23A-27-14, a court shall order that all official records ... be sealed'. Sealing is the court's obligation on discharge and dismissal; no participant motion exists. The unresolved fallback unit is therefore not carried, and the track is modelled as process_guidance. What remains open is the remedy where a court did not do what the statute directs.": "Q-044",
    "va_exp_absolute_pardon||Chapter 23.2 and \u00a7 19.2-392.2 both carry future versions that the controlling review predates and does not record. Read at source on 2026-08-06: \u00a7 19.2-392.2 exists in two versions, one effective until December 1, 2026 and one effective December 1, 2026, the later of which broadens subsection A to any person 'arrested, charged, summonsed, or indicted' who 'is not ultimately convicted' provided no stipulation or judicial finding of facts sufficient to find guilt and no deferred adjudication, codifies the Dotson limit in the statute itself, and adds a new subsection C sentence permitting a single petition to cover multiple charges arising out of SEPARATE transactions or occurrences. Section 19.2-392.6 likewise carries an A subsection effective until July 1, 2027 and a second effective July 1, 2027, and its historical citation now includes 2026, Sp. Sess. I, c. 1, which the review did not have. The review's open question about a 2026 amendment is therefore answered in the affirmative. Every Virginia track must be re-read before 2026-12-01 and again before 2027-07-01.": "Q-045",
    "va_seal_ancillary_matter_only||Chapter 23.2 and \u00a7 19.2-392.2 both carry future versions that the controlling review predates and does not record. Read at source on 2026-08-06: \u00a7 19.2-392.2 exists in two versions, one effective until December 1, 2026 and one effective December 1, 2026, the later of which broadens subsection A to any person 'arrested, charged, summonsed, or indicted' who 'is not ultimately convicted' provided no stipulation or judicial finding of facts sufficient to find guilt and no deferred adjudication, codifies the Dotson limit in the statute itself, and adds a new subsection C sentence permitting a single petition to cover multiple charges arising out of SEPARATE transactions or occurrences. Section 19.2-392.6 likewise carries an A subsection effective until July 1, 2027 and a second effective July 1, 2027, and its historical citation now includes 2026, Sp. Sess. I, c. 1, which the review did not have. The review's open question about a 2026 amendment is therefore answered in the affirmative. Every Virginia track must be re-read before 2026-12-01 and again before 2027-07-01.": "Q-046",
    "wv_acc_treatment_job_readiness||How the \u00a7 61-11-26a(a)(1) 90-day single-misdemeanour clock interacts with the \u00a7 61-11-26(b)(1) one-year clock where the participant relies on job readiness graduation rather than 90 days of treatment compliance. Subdivision (a)(1) was read at source on 2026-08-06 and makes eligibility turn on '90 days' of programme compliance 'or upon completion of an approved job readiness adult training course, or both, if applicable, but after the completion of any sentence of incarceration or completion of any period of supervision, whichever is later in time'. On the graduation branch there is no stated period at all beyond completion of sentence and supervision, which would make the route available immediately on graduation. The packet applies the text as written and records the question rather than reading a 90-day period into the graduation branch.": "Q-047",
    "wv_common_conv_procedure||Venue on a multi-county conviction petition. The controlling review states that S.B. 562 (2020) allows a person seeking expungement of convictions in more than one county to file in his or her county of residence. Section 61-11-26 was read in full at source on 2026-08-06 and the current text does not contain a residence-county venue option: subsections (a)(1) and (a)(2) both say 'petition the circuit court in which the conviction or convictions occurred', the subsection (d) proviso requires a multiple-misdemeanour petition to 'identify and group such information by circuit court, as applicable', and \u00a7 61-11-26a(a) says 'may petition the circuit court or circuit courts in which the conviction or convictions occurred'. Read together the current text supports one petition covering several counties, grouped by circuit court and served on the prosecuting attorney of each county of conviction, but it does not name a residence-county forum. Venue for the single-county case is unaffected and unambiguous. Until counsel resolves this, the packet directs a multi-county participant to the circuit court of conviction and adds a local-check instruction rather than promising a residence-county filing.": "Q-048",
    "wv_conv_multiple_misdemeanors||Where a multiple-misdemeanour group spans more than one county, which circuit court receives the single petition. Section 61-11-26 was read in full at source on 2026-08-06: subsection (a)(1) says 'the circuit court in which the conviction or convictions occurred', and the subsection (d) proviso requires the petition to 'identify and group such information by circuit court, as applicable'. Together those support one petition covering several counties, but the text does not say which of the several circuit courts is the forum, and it does not contain the county-of-residence option the controlling review attributes to S.B. 562. Until counsel answers, the packet directs the participant to the circuit court of conviction and, for a genuinely multi-county group, to a lawyer.": "Q-049",
    "wv_conv_multiple_misdemeanors||Whether an excluded conviction sitting inside an otherwise eligible multiple-misdemeanour group can be severed, so the remaining convictions are expunged, or whether its presence in the petition risks the whole petition being summarily denied under \u00a7 61-11-26(i)(4). The burden at subsection (h)(1) is framed per conviction, which suggests severance, but the once-per-lifetime rule makes the downside of getting it wrong permanent.": "Q-050",
    "wv_conv_nonviolent_felony||How LegalEase should present a route it may generate but on which it cannot make the central characterisation. Section 61-11-26(p)(5) was read at source on 2026-08-06 and makes two of the four limbs of 'nonviolent felony' express circuit-court findings, so the eligibility recital on SCA-C907 is one LegalEase must leave for the participant and their counsel. The controlling review calls for a manual-review gate on every felony. LegalEase operates no review queue, no staff approval and no eligibility determination, so that direction is normalized here as a printed attorney-referral notice and a self-help boundary rather than as an internal gate. Whether counsel accepts that translation, or would rather the felony route not be offered at all until a referral partner is in place, is a question for counsel.": "Q-051",
    "wv_conv_single_misdemeanor||Which published form is the operative \u00a7 61-11-26 misdemeanour petition. The Judiciary Court Forms index was read at source on 2026-08-06 and publishes exactly five expungement forms. SCA-C900, captioned 'Instructions for Expungement of Records Petition' and revised 04/01/2021, is a five-page document whose pages 2 to 5 are themselves a petition, verification and certificate of service. That embedded petition is materially stale: it requires the petitioner to have been 'between eighteen and twenty-six years of age', requires 'no prior or subsequent convictions other than minor traffic violations', recites 'W. Va. Code \u00a761-11-26 (2009)', and cross-references the exclusion list to '\u00a761-11-26(i)', all of which are features of the pre-2019 section. The section in force places exclusions at subsection (c) and imposes no age limit. SCA-C906 matches the current subsection (c) and (d) structure and carries the \u00a7 61-11-26a elections, and is therefore used here. The packet must not generate the SCA-C900 embedded petition. Whether the Judiciary intends SCA-C900 to remain published in that form is not established.": "Q-052",
    "wv_conv_single_misdemeanor||The SCA-C900 instruction sheet, revised 04/01/2021, tells the petitioner that after a notice of opposition they 'will then have ten (10) days of being served of such notice to reply', and cites \u00a7 61-11-26(e) as the opposition provision. Section 61-11-26 was read in full at source on 2026-08-06: opposition is at subsection (g), and subsection (g)(3) gives the petitioner 30 days after service of the opposition to reply. The packet states the statutory 30 days and flags the discrepancy, but which the circuits apply in practice is not established, and a participant who relies on the form's ten days would not be prejudiced while one who relies on thirty might be.": "Q-053",
    "wv_nc_acquittal_dismissal||SCA-C903 was read at source on 2026-08-06. Its recital is to 'W.Va. Code \u00a7 61-11-25 (2000)', and the form's stated revision date is 04/01/2010, so the published form predates the 2010, 2012, 2022, 2023 and 2024 amendments to the section, including the H.B. 4399 (2024) text now in force. The form's operative paragraphs still track the current subsection (a) requirements for a straight acquittal or dismissal, so the fit is sound for this track, but whether the Judiciary intends the 2000 recital to be corrected, and whether any circuit rejects the form on that basis, is not established.": "Q-054",
    "wv_nc_diversion_deferred||Whether SCA-C903 is the right vehicle for a deferred-adjudication dismissal. The form was read at source on 2026-08-06: it is captioned 'for Reasons Other than Entry of a Plea', recites 'W.Va. Code \u00a7 61-11-25 (2000)', carries a single charge block with one free-text reason-for-dismissal field, has no same-transaction-or-occurrence paragraph and no diversion or deferred-adjudication paragraph, and requires the movant to state that the dismissal 'was not in exchange for a guilty plea to another offense'. Section 61-11-22a operates on an entered guilty plea which is withdrawn on compliance, so the movant's statement is literally true \u2014 the dismissal is not in exchange for a plea to another offence \u2014 but the caption and the missing paragraphs make the fit imperfect. The packet therefore generates the official form plus a supplemental pleading carrying the statutory basis, the same-transaction recital and the carve-out recital. Whether the Judiciary regards SCA-C903 as the correct form for this route, or whether a distinct form or a full custom petition is expected, is not established.": "Q-055",
    "wv_pardon_expungement||Whether \u00a7 5-1-16a(b) is the whole of the effect of a pardon expungement. The subsection was read at source on 2026-08-06 and provides only that the expunged record may not be considered in an application to a West Virginia educational institution or in an application for a licence required by a West Virginia professional organisation. Unlike \u00a7\u00a7 61-11-25(e) and 61-11-26(l), it contains no general non-disclosure provision and no statement that the proceedings are considered never to have occurred. What the packet may tell a participant about disclosing the record to an ordinary employer after a \u00a7 5-1-16a expungement is therefore not settled.": "Q-056"
  }
}